  "answer_text": "The iconic scene of Del Boy falling through the bar is from the episode **\"Yuppy Love\"**! Here are all the details:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcfa Episode: *\"Yuppy Love\"* \u2014 Series 6, Episode 1\n**\"Yuppy Love\"** was the first episode of Series 6 of *Only Fools and Horses*, airing on **8th January 1989** on BBC1, pulling in an impressive **13.9 million viewers**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf7a The Scene\nIn a wine bar, Del is leaning against a folding section at the end of the bar. He spots an attractive young woman looking at him. A barman comes along and lifts up the section Del was leaning against to get through, but doesn't put it back down \u2014 leaving a gap behind Del's back, which he doesn't notice at all. Del then says *\"Play it nice and cool, son. Nice and cool, you know what I mean\u2026\"* \u2014 and leans back, falling straight through the gap onto the floor.\n\nJust as funny as the fall itself was **Trigger's baffled reaction** to Del's sudden disappearance.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfad Behind the Scenes\n\n- **Real-life inspiration:** The scene was inspired by writer **John Sullivan** watching a man do the exact same thing in a real wine bar, except the man grabbed onto the fixed part of the bar so he didn't fall all the way over. Sullivan thought it was funny watching the man's body language as he tried to recover his cool.\n\n- **How the fall was performed:** Sullivan originally wanted a slip, stumble, and tree-like fall, but **David Jason** thought Del should go all the way over \u2014 starting to go sideways, then falling over without looking in the direction of the fall, which Jason felt was key to the scene. There was a hidden crash mat, but it was a difficult shot because it was hard not to look where he was falling. Jason's experience doing falls in the theatre helped.\n\n- **Trigger wasn't meant to be there:** A rehearsal script that went up for auction revealed that **Trigger wasn't originally supposed to be in the scene**. However, Roger Lloyd-Pack happened to be there on the day, and John Sullivan decided to include him. Fellow actor John Challis (who played Boycie) confirmed the story was true, saying they took advantage of Lloyd-Pack being around at the time of filming.\n\n- **Trigger's reaction was genuine:** Roger Lloyd-Pack revealed that Jason never did the fall during rehearsals, so when it happened it was a genuine surprise \u2014 making it very hard to keep a straight face under the pressure of the studio recording.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfc6 Legacy\nDel Boy's fall through the bar has become **one of the most celebrated sitcom scenes ever**. David Jason says he gets asked about the fall all the time, and is happy to be remembered for something so iconic.",
